    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <p><strong>Position:</strong> In my view, OEM battery manufacturing is not buying a product — it is commissioning a repeatable supply arrangement, and the six lines of a quote decide the price before the total does.</p>
      <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>The five parameters — standard family, case group, electrical spec, terminal and technology — must be fixed together, or the quote is a guess.</li>
        <li>Two factories can quote the 'same battery' US$8 apart, and the difference is almost never profit — it is scope, on the CCA basis, tooling and packaging.</li>
        <li>The specification lock — sample plus written parameters plus change control — is what retires the drift risk on every later container.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><em>Disclosure: this is the author's editorial judgment and field experience, not a verified technical claim.</em></p>
